        <h1>How to Choose a Tuition Centre in Singapore: A Parent&rsquo;s Guide</h1>
        <div class="blog-post-meta">
          <a class="blog-author" href="/about/" rel="author">By Jeremy Lim, Founder</a>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <time datetime="2026-05-27">27 May 2026</time>
          <span>&middot;</span>
          <span class="blog-reading-time">7 min read</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="blog-post-body">
        <p>Almost every tuition centre in Singapore says the same things: experienced tutors, proven results, small classes. So the hard part for a parent is telling them apart. The right fit can lift a grade and rebuild confidence, while the wrong one costs months you don&rsquo;t have before the O- or A-Levels. Here are seven things that separate a good centre from an expensive one, and the red flags worth walking away from.</p>

        <h2>1. Class size, and whether it&rsquo;s real</h2>
        <p>&ldquo;Small classes&rdquo; means nothing without a number: a class of 6 and a class of 20 are very different things. In a small group your child&rsquo;s work is seen and corrected every session. In a large one they can sit unnoticed for weeks. Ask for the <em>maximum</em> class size, not the average, and ask how often the tutor reviews each student&rsquo;s written work. If the answer is vague, assume the classes are bigger than they sound.</p>

        <h2>2. A method, not more worksheets</h2>
        <p>Many centres sell volume: more practice papers, more drilling. A child who keeps making the same mistakes needs a <em>method</em>, not more repetitions of the wrong technique. Ask how the centre teaches a student to approach a question they&rsquo;ve never seen before. A good answer describes a repeatable thinking process. A weak one promises &ldquo;lots of practice&rdquo;. The difference shows when the exam question doesn&rsquo;t match anything in the practice stack.</p>

        <h2>3. Who actually teaches your child</h2>
        <p>Ask who will be in front of your child each week. Is it the experienced tutor named in the marketing, or a rotating cast of part-timers? Continuity matters: a tutor who knows your child&rsquo;s weak spots across the term will move them faster than four different relief tutors. Ask about the tutor&rsquo;s background too.</p>

        <h2>4. Proof, not claims</h2>
        <p>Every centre advertises results. The credible ones can show you <em>specific</em> cases, such as a student who moved from C5 to A2 and how it happened, rather than a wall of distinction percentages with no context. Be sceptical of figures with no story behind them, and ask to see or hear about students whose starting point resembled your child&rsquo;s.</p>

        <h2>5. Transparent, all-inclusive fees</h2>
        <p>Hidden costs are common: registration fees, material fees, deposits, and long lock-in contracts that make leaving expensive. A centre that&rsquo;s confident in its teaching will publish its fees, charge month to month and include materials. If you have to email for a price, or you&rsquo;re asked to commit to a year upfront, treat both as warning signs.</p>

        <h2>6. A real way to try before you commit</h2>
        <p>You shouldn&rsquo;t have to gamble months of fees to find out whether a centre fits. A free diagnostic assessment lets the centre show, rather than tell, what it would do for your child. A centre that won&rsquo;t let you see anything before you pay is asking for a lot of trust it hasn&rsquo;t earned yet.</p>

        <h2>7. Fit with your child</h2>
        <p>Finally, the part you can&rsquo;t read off a brochure: does your child come out of a lesson clearer and a little more confident, or just more tired? A centre that looks perfect on paper is the wrong one if your child dreads going. Watch how they feel after the first few lessons. That tells you more than any sales conversation.</p>

        <h2>Red flags to walk away from</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Guaranteed grades.</strong> No honest tutor can promise an A. Learning doesn&rsquo;t work that way.</li>
          <li><strong>Pressure to sign a long contract</strong> before your child has attended a single lesson.</li>
          <li><strong>Vague answers</strong> about class size, who teaches, or what the actual fee is.</li>
          <li><strong>&ldquo;Just do more practice&rdquo;</strong> as the entire strategy, with no method behind it.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>A simple way to decide</h2>
        <p>Shortlist two or three centres, ask the questions above, and book a free assessment at each. The assessments show you which centre understands your child&rsquo;s gaps, and the first few lessons show you which one your child responds to. Pick the place where your child starts to improve, not the one with the glossiest brochure.</p>
        <p>Here&rsquo;s how A-Worthy answers the same questions. Groups are capped at 6. We teach the SHARP Method, a step-by-step way into each type of exam question, instead of piling on worksheets. The same tutor teaches your child every week. Your child gets written feedback on their work every week, and you get a progress report by email each month.</p>
        <p>Our fees are published for every level, from SGD 280 a month. There&rsquo;s no registration fee, our own notes and practice papers are included, and you give one month&rsquo;s notice to stop. The assessment is free, and the first month of lessons comes with a money-back guarantee.</p>
